33
00:02:42,289 --> 00:02:45,629
Now we demonstrate Role-Based Access Control.

34
00:02:45,779 --> 00:02:51,346
Invoking the protected user endpoint at GET /api/user/profile with our

35
00:02:51,496 --> 00:02:57,063
Bearer token returns HTTP 200 OK with authorized profile details.

36
00:02:57,213 --> 00:03:03,337
However, when we attempt to access GET /api/admin/dashboard using this same

37
00:03:03,487 --> 00:03:10,167
token, Spring Security blocks the request, returning an immediate HTTP 403 Forbidden.

38
00:03:10,317 --> 00:03:16,441
This confirms strict privilege boundary separation between standard users and administrators.

39
00:03:16,591 --> 00:03:20,488
To balance security with usability, short-lived access

40
00:03:20,638 --> 00:03:24,535
tokens are refreshed using persistent refresh tokens.

41
00:03:24,685 --> 00:03:33,592
When we invoke POST /api/auth/refresh, the backend executes strict token rotation: the submitted refresh token is

42
00:03:33,742 --> 00:03:42,649
permanently deleted from PostgreSQL, and a brand new refresh token is issued alongside a fresh JWT.

43
00:03:42,799 --> 00:03:49,480
If an attacker replays the old token, the backend detects it is

44
00:03:49,630 --> 00:03:56,867
missing and rejects the request with HTTP 401 Unauthorized, defeating token replay attacks.

45
00:03:57,017 --> 00:04:00,914
Password recovery enforces anti-enumeration by returning a

46
00:04:01,064 --> 00:04:04,960
uniform success message regardless of account existence.

47
00:04:05,110 --> 00:04:09,007
Validating the 1-hour reset token updates the

48
00:04:09,157 --> 00:04:13,054
BCrypt password hash and nullifies the token.

49
00:04:13,204 --> 00:04:18,771
Crucially, as verified in commit 1e27b90, password reset instantly purges

50
00:04:18,921 --> 00:04:25,045
all active refresh tokens from PostgreSQL, terminating sessions across all devices.

51
00:04:25,195 --> 00:04:30,762
Similarly, invoking Logout purges the user's refresh token from the

52
00:04:30,912 --> 00:04:36,479
database, preventing subsequent token renewals while stateless JWTs expire naturally.

